Bradycardia
Cardiovascular
Heart rate <60 bpm, Lightheadedness/Syncope, Poor exercise tolerance, Slow rhythm on ECG

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-007
Reason for Visit: Symptomatic low heart rate causing presyncope and acute mobility restriction.
Location: Aged care facility
Present at Visit: Patient and nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage by facility staff reported a sudden decline in patient alertness, unsteadiness, and a recorded heart rate in the 40s. Urgent medical assessment was necessary to evaluate for high-degree atrioventricular block or medication toxicity to prevent complete cardiovascular collapse or fall-related trauma prior to the next morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I feel incredibly weak, dizzy when I try to stand, and my chest feels heavy.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports profound lethargy and lightheadedness worsening over the past 24 hours. Experienced near-syncope upon attempting to transfer from bed to chair. Denies true loss of consciousness. Notes generalized poor exercise tolerance for several weeks, now acutely worsened. Caregivers report the patient appeared briefly confused this afternoon. No acute diaphoresis.
Functional Status: Normally mobilizes with a frame; currently bedbound due to dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme fall risk secondary to cerebral hypoperfusion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/55 | HR: 42 | Temp: 36.4 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Pale, lethargic, slow to respond but oriented to person and place.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals profound bradycardia. Rhythm is regular but notably slow. No murmurs auscultated. Jugular venous pressure is normal. Lungs are clear. Neurological exam reveals no focal motor or sensory deficits, but mentation is sluggish. Capillary refill is 3 seconds.
Environmental Observations: Bed rails up; mobility aids out of reach to discourage unassisted movement.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient is taking Metoprolol 50mg twice daily and Digoxin 125mcg daily.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Bradycardia - Status: Exacerbated (Symptomatic, likely iatrogenic)
Differentials:

Acute Myocardial Infarction (Inferior) - Ruled out/unlikely due to absence of acute ischemic chest pain, nausea, or profound diaphoresis.

Hypothyroidism - Ruled out/unlikely due to the acute nature of the severe symptoms and absence of myxedema signs.

Vasovagal Response - Ruled out/unlikely due to persistent, non-resolving low heart rate regardless of posture.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is exhibiting highly symptomatic bradycardia with hypotension and signs of poor cerebral perfusion (lethargy, presyncope). The physical exam confirms a heart rate of 42 bpm in the context of nodal-blocking medications. This objective evidence of hemodynamic compromise indicates an impending risk of syncope or complete heart block. Urgent after-hours assessment and intervention were strictly necessary to manage the acute iatrogenic risk and stabilize the patient, fulfilling MBS criteria for urgent clinical necessity.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Withhold all doses of Metoprolol and Digoxin immediately. Recommend urgent transfer via non-emergency ambulance to the emergency department for 12-lead ECG, telemetry monitoring, and evaluation of medication toxicity vs. intrinsic conduction disease.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent falls, do not attempt to stand or walk under any circumstances. Request nursing assistance for all needs until heart rate is stabilized in the hospital.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover provided to transferring ambulance crew and receiving ED triage.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate 000 upgrade if the patient loses consciousness, seizes, or systolic blood pressure drops below 85 mmHg while awaiting transport.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The exact cause of the slow heart rate requires an ECG and blood tests in the hospital.
Clinical Course: The heart is beating too slowly to pump enough blood to the brain. Medication adjustment or a pacemaker may be required depending on the hospital findings.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Remain strictly flat in bed. Do not sit up. Inform nursing staff immediately if vision darkens or chest pain begins.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your heart rate medications have built up and are slowing your heart too much. You need hospital monitoring to safely let these wear off.
Actionable Steps: Keep completely still. Allow the paramedics to move you. Do not take any further heart pills until seen by the hospital doctor.

